<%# `:secondary`. **Irreversible is not the same as destructive**, and this control had it both ways:
    the note below already argues that the dialog's accept button stays the brand primary because
    "finalizing is irreversible and it is not destructive: it pays students and locks entries, and
    nothing is lost" - while the trigger sat on `:danger_outline` and lit up rose under the pointer.
    Reported as exactly that: it "lights up like a destructive button on hover. This should not be the
    case."

    The rose hover is the affordance for an action that *takes something away* - delete, deactivate,
    archive - and every other `:danger_outline` in this app is one of those. Finalizing adds money to
    portfolios. Stripe's "Pay $X" is not a red button, and design.md reserves the rose fill for a
    destructive confirmation.

    What carries the weight instead is what should: the label, the amount stated on the card above, the
    two-part confirmation below, and Cancel holding `autofocus`. Colour was never doing that work - at
    rest `:danger_outline` and `:secondary` are the same button, so the only reader who saw a warning was
    one already hovering the control they had decided to press.

    It stays `:secondary` rather than becoming the page's primary because "Save grades" is the primary
    here, which is the hierarchy this page was rebuilt around.

    **The confirmation is two parts, which is what the dialog is built for.** It passed one string -
    "Finalize and pay $12.00 to 2 students? This cannot be undone." - so the whole sentence landed in the
    title and the body stayed hidden. `shared/_confirm_dialog` splits a message on its first blank line
    into the question and the consequence, and its own note says why: a one-line confirmation can only
    restate the button, and macOS, Polaris and GOV.UK all put the consequence in the body.

    The consequence comes from `finalize_consequence`, the same helper the card above renders, so the
    preview and the confirmation cannot describe one payment two ways.

    **The accept button is the brand primary, not `.tw-btn-danger`** - for the same reason as the trigger
    above, and it was already right when the trigger was not.

    An earlier note here claimed `turbo_confirm` had no override and was a native OS dialog. That stopped
    being true when `confirm_dialog_controller` took over `Turbo.config.forms.confirm`.

    No disabled variant: the block around this is not rendered for a completed grade book, which has
    already paid out, so there is no dead control to grey.

    **Not an autosave target.** It carried `autosave_target: "button"`, and the autosave timer clicks
    `buttonTarget` - the *first* matching target in the DOM. That was "Save grades" only because this card
    renders after the table; moving it above would have had the timer pay the students every thirty
    seconds. The turbo_stream still replaces this by id, which is all it needed. %>
<%# The blank line is the channel: Turbo copies exactly five data attributes onto the form it builds for
    a link and passes no submitter for one, so a second attribute would reach nothing. See the dialog. %>
<% confirm = "Finalize these grades?\n\n#{finalize_consequence(earnings, @grade_book)}" %>

<%= button_to "Finalize grades", finalize_classroom_grade_book_path(@classroom, @grade_book),
              class: "tw-btn-secondary",
              data: { turbo_frame: "_top", turbo_confirm: confirm },
              form: { id: "finalize-button" } %>
